{
  "version": 1,
  "id": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000103",
  "title": "Sierpinski Triangle",
  "created_at": "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z",
  "updated_at": "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z",
  "shared_cargo_toml": "[profile.release]\nopt-level = 1\nlto = false\ncodegen-units = 16",
  "cells": [
    {
      "id": "cell_0",
      "order": 0,
      "label": "Introduction",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "# Sierpinski Triangle \u25b2\n\nThe **Sierpinski triangle** is a fractal that emerges from a surprisingly simple random process called the **chaos game**.\n\n## The Chaos Game\n\n1. Pick three vertices of an equilateral triangle\n2. Start at any point in the plane\n3. Repeat:\n   - Randomly choose one of the three vertices\n   - Move **halfway** toward that vertex\n   - Plot your new position\n\nDespite the randomness, the plotted points converge to a self-similar fractal \u2014 the Sierpinski triangle \u2014 with Hausdorff dimension $\\log 3 / \\log 2 \\approx 1.585$.",
      "cargo_toml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_1",
      "order": 1,
      "label": "Chaos Game",
      "cell_type": "Code",
      "source": "// Vertices of an equilateral triangle (apex at top in Cartesian coords)\nlet h = 3.0_f64.sqrt() / 2.0;\nlet vertices: [(f64, f64); 3] = [\n    (0.5, h),   // top\n    (0.0, 0.0), // bottom-left\n    (1.0, 0.0), // bottom-right\n];\n\n// Deterministic PRNG (LCG) \u2014 no external crate needed\nstruct Rng(u64);\nimpl Rng {\n    fn next_vertex(&mut self) -> usize {\n        self.0 = self.0\n            .wrapping_mul(6_364_136_223_846_793_005)\n            .wrapping_add(1_442_695_040_888_963_407);\n        ((self.0 >> 33) % 3) as usize\n    }\n}\nlet mut rng = Rng(42);\n\n// Start at the centroid\nlet mut x = (vertices[0].0 + vertices[1].0 + vertices[2].0) / 3.0;\nlet mut y = (vertices[0].1 + vertices[1].1 + vertices[2].1) / 3.0;\n\n// Run the chaos game: pick a random vertex, move halfway toward it\nlet num_points = 50_000_usize;\nlet mut points = Vec::with_capacity(num_points);\nfor _ in 0..num_points {\n    let v = vertices[rng.next_vertex()];\n    x = (x + v.0) / 2.0;\n    y = (y + v.1) / 2.0;\n    points.push((x, y));\n}\n\npoints\n",
      "cargo_toml": null,
      "collapsed": true
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_2",
      "order": 2,
      "label": "Render Fractal",
      "cell_type": "Code",
      "source": "use ironpad_cell::prelude::*;\n\n// Receive the chaos game points from the previous cell\nlet points: Vec<(f64, f64)> = cell1;\nlet y_max = 3.0_f64.sqrt() / 2.0;\n\nlet canvas_w = 800u32;\nlet canvas_h = 600u32;\nlet margin = 0.05_f64;\nlet x_range = 1.0 + 2.0 * margin;\nlet y_range = y_max + 2.0 * margin;\n\nlet mut canvas = Canvas::new(canvas_w, canvas_h);\ncanvas.fill((10, 10, 20));\n\nfor &(x, y) in &points {\n    let px_f = (x + margin) / x_range * canvas_w as f64;\n    let py_f = (1.0 - (y + margin) / y_range) * canvas_h as f64;\n    if px_f >= 0.0 && px_f < canvas_w as f64 && py_f >= 0.0 && py_f < canvas_h as f64 {\n        canvas.set_pixel(px_f as u32, py_f as u32, (50, 205, 50));\n    }\n}\n\nCellOutput::from(canvas)\n",
      "cargo_toml": null,
      "collapsed": true
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_3",
      "order": 3,
      "label": "Why Chaos Produces Order",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "## Why Does Chaos Produce Order?\n\nThe chaos game works because of **iterated function systems** (IFS). Each \"move halfway to a vertex\" is a contraction mapping that shrinks the plane by a factor of $\\tfrac{1}{2}$. The Sierpinski triangle is the unique *attractor* of these three contractions \u2014 regardless of the starting point, the orbit converges to the same fractal.\n\n### Variations to Try\n\n- **Different ratios**: Move $\\tfrac{1}{3}$ of the way instead of $\\tfrac{1}{2}$ \u2014 what happens?\n- **More vertices**: Use 4 vertices (square) with the constraint that you can't pick the same vertex twice in a row\n- **Weighted randomness**: Make one vertex more likely than the others \u2014 the triangle becomes asymmetric\n\nThe chaos game reveals a deep connection between randomness and deterministic geometry: the fractal is encoded in the *rules*, not the *randomness*.",
      "cargo_toml": null
    }
  ],
  "description": "Recursively subdivide a triangle to produce the classic Sierpinski fractal pattern.",
  "tags": [
    "fractal",
    "visualization",
    "recursion"
  ]
}